The four findings A1.9 must fix, from docs/reviews/sprint-1024-claude.log lines
309-328, quoted so the next reader does not have to re-derive them:
  B7-L1 Prove-it is not implemented as specified: nothing presents a problem
        from mathd/corpus or reads the owner's answer; the CLI verifies claims
        typed in full or replays a fixture.
  B7-L2 There is no explicit reveal command, separate from Prove-it.
  B7-L3 The ledger records only Failed claims, with no problem id, verdict or
        time taken.
  B7-L4 mathd/tests/check_b7_ledger.py, the independent oracle, is run by no
        workflow or script.
  B7-L5 (owner) the twenty-problem session: not an agent's to close.

What this review will do when the commits land, so the work is not re-planned
from scratch:
  1. For each of L1-L4, name the commit and the file, and check the finding's
     exact complaint, not the commit message: a Prove-it command that reads
     mathd/corpus and prompts for an answer; a separate reveal command; a ledger
     entry carrying problem id, answer, verdict, timestamp and elapsed time;
     check_b7_ledger.py wired into a workflow or scripts/check.sh.
  2. Plant at least two faults of my own: a ledger that writes a Verified-only
     entry with the verdict field dropped, and a UUID/timestamp recomputation
     that the Python oracle must catch. A plant that the oracle misses is a
     finding.
  3. Read one test closely: what is on each side, and where each side came from.
     The oracle must be CPython json/hashlib, not the crate's own code.
  4. For A1.10, re-run at least five mutants from the tool's fixed list myself
     and compare killed/survived against the committed log. A survivor is a
     finding for the lead; a mutant that the log calls killed but that I can
     show surviving reopens the item.

